<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Suppression policy. Every <suppress> element must carry all three of:

    - the CVE it suppresses, by id. Never a bare path or package glob, which
      would also hide findings nobody has looked at yet, including ones that
      appear months later in the same dependency.
    - an owner, named. "The team" is nobody, and an unowned suppression is how a
      known vulnerability quietly becomes permanent.
    - an expiry, via the until= attribute. The finding reopens on that date if
      nothing was done, which is the difference between accepting a risk and
      forgetting it.

  A suppression missing any of the three does not belong here. Empty is the
  correct state until a finding has been triaged and the reasoning written down
  in the security assessment.

  Add an entry only after deciding, in writing, why the finding does not apply
  to this application - not because a build went red on a Friday.

  ===========================================================================
  Triage of 2026-08-26, against Spring Boot 3.5.16 with tomcat.version 10.1.59.

  The scan found 73 findings at CVSS >= 7.0 on Spring Boot 3.3.5. Upgrading to
  3.5.16 cleared every Spring, Jackson, Kafka and PostgreSQL finding and left 11.
  Overriding tomcat.version to 10.1.59 cleared the remaining six Tomcat findings.
  Five remain, and all five are suppressed below - not because they are
  inconvenient, but because in each case the vulnerable code is not on this
  application's classpath. Both rationales are checkable with dependency:tree.

  Nothing here is accepted-but-exploitable. If that changes, it belongs in
  docs/risk-register.md under Accepted risks, not in this file.
  ===========================================================================
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

  <!--
    CVE-2025-7962 - angus-activation-2.0.3.jar
    Owner: Luke Pupilli. Reviewed 2026-08-26.

    Two independent reasons, either sufficient.

    Wrong package. The advisory reads "In Jakarta Mail versions prior to 2.0.2 it
    is possible to perform an SMTP Injection". Jakarta Mail is angus-mail;
    angus-activation is the Jakarta Activation implementation, a different
    artifact from the same Eclipse project. Dependency-Check has matched on the
    shared "angus" CPE.

    Wrong application. This service sends no mail. There is no
    spring-boot-starter-mail and no jakarta.mail on the classpath, so there is no
    SMTP path to inject into. angus-activation arrives as a runtime transitive of
    jaxb-core via jaxb-runtime, for XML binding.

    Verify with: ./mvnw dependency:tree -Dincludes="org.eclipse.angus:*"
  -->
  <suppress until="2026-11-26Z">
    <notes>SMTP injection in Jakarta Mail. This is angus-activation, not angus-mail, and this service sends no mail. Owner: Luke Pupilli.</notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.eclipse\.angus/angus-activation@.*$</packageUrl>
    <cve>CVE-2025-7962</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    CVE-2026-34478, CVE-2026-34479, CVE-2026-34480, CVE-2026-34481
    log4j-api-2.24.3.jar. Owner: Luke Pupilli. Reviewed 2026-08-26.

    All four are defects in Log4j *Core* layouts - Rfc5424Layout, Log4j1XmlLayout,
    XmlLayout and JsonTemplateLayout by name in the advisories. Those classes ship
    in log4j-core, which is not on this classpath.

    What is present is log4j-api, pulled in only under log4j-to-slf4j:

        \- org.apache.logging.log4j:log4j-to-slf4j:jar:2.24.3:compile
           \- org.apache.logging.log4j:log4j-api:jar:2.24.3:compile

    log4j-to-slf4j is a bridge: it routes calls made against the Log4j API into
    SLF4J, which Spring Boot binds to Logback. No Log4j layout is ever
    instantiated, because no Log4j implementation is present to instantiate one.
    The console pattern in application.yml is Logback configuration.

    Dependency-Check flags these because log4j-api carries the same
    "apache:log4j" CPE as log4j-core and the version falls in the affected range.

    This suppression stops applying the moment log4j-core appears on the
    classpath. Re-triage if it ever does.

    Verify with: ./mvnw dependency:tree -Dincludes="org.apache.logging.log4j:*"
  -->
  <suppress until="2026-11-26Z">
    <notes>Log4j Core layout defects. Only log4j-api is present, via log4j-to-slf4j; log4j-core is absent and logging goes through Logback. Owner: Luke Pupilli.</notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.logging\.log4j/log4j-api@.*$</packageUrl>
    <cve>CVE-2026-34478</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-34479</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-34480</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-34481</cve>
  </suppress>

</suppressions>
